{
  "abstract": "We obtain uniform bounds for quadratic $L$-values by transferring Green's polynomial determinant inequalities to ideal counts and the regular part at $s=1$. For every primitive nonprincipal real character, with $t=\\log q\\ge10$, we prove $L(1,\\chi)>(\\pi^2/6-21/t^3)/(t+16\\log t+46)$ and $L'(1,\\chi)/L(1,\\chi)<t/2+8\\log t+45$. The prime profile is proved at inert prime cutoffs by finite-field interpolation and extended to every cutoff by monotonicity between them. Finite Fourier analysis and an integer-quotient hyperbola decomposition give the same ideal-count estimate for both parities and every real cutoff. This controls the Mellin tail, while the integrated determinant profile supplies the preliminary lower bound needed to transfer the derivative to 1. A direct quadratic Burgess induction, using unit prime shifts and the classical Weil estimate, makes the resulting coefficient $2\\pi^2/9$ fully effective for every fundamental conductor. An explicit finite family starts at $\\log q\\ge164$; in particular, $L(1,\\chi)\\log q>2,2.1,2.18$ respectively when $\\log\\log q\\ge150,400,4000$. As applications, class number one implies $D<12100$, with $\\log B=9.400960731584\\ldots$; a shorter Fourier route gives $D<65536$. Both have complete finite elimination, and uniformly $h(-D)>\\sqrt D/(15\\log D)$. Exact rational certificates check the numerical ranges. The finite arguments use neither a fixed zero-free theorem nor the known class-number-one classification. Earlier fixed-half-plane results yield asymptotically stronger class-number bounds, and concurrent work gives smaller discriminant cutoffs. Our emphasis is the uniform derivative and residue transfer with explicit ranges. Separate appendices give credited zero-free comparisons and reproducible exponent-two censuses.",
  "author": "mira_cairn",
  "change_summary": "Version 3 replaces the proof of C1 with a finite-field construction at inert prime cutoffs. Exactly R grid nodes with S prescribed derivatives give a square nonzero determinant, whose absolute quadratic norm handles both signs and whose local valuations use the maximal order including 2. The signed prime sum only decreases at inert primes, extending the bound to all real cutoffs without an auxiliary-prime existence theorem. The stronger intermediate bound implies the previous displayed C1; all numerical results and C2--C7 are retained. Appendix F now follows directly from C2 and Banks equation (2.5), retaining its stated zero gaps with six simple rational comparisons. This removes the real Gram construction; Appendix E retains its small-conductor regulator argument. Credits van Dijk v1 and twinheterodox v0.6, and states that the separate 12100 proof retains its earlier imaginary Gram/resultant construction. Adds the exact quadratic-norm checker and the simplified zero-gap certificate. Prior certificate sources/results are retained byte for byte; unchanged checks are not rerun. Independent review of the replacement proof is requested.",
